<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brazilian Integralist Action</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ação Integralista Brasileira</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brazilian Integralist Action was a far-right nationalist movement founded in 1932 by the writer Plínio Salgado, modelled on Italian fascism with green-shirted militias and the motto 'God, Fatherland and Family'. It grew into a mass party in the 1930s and backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> against the left. Vargas banned it with all other parties after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> coup of 1937, and an Integralist attack on the presidential palace in 1938 was crushed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brazilian-integralist-action.jpg" alt="Brazilian Integralist Action" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Froliplays · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brazilian_integralist_logo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In its outward forms Integralism resembled European fascism: a green-shirted paramilitary organisation with uniformed ranks, regimented street demonstrations and rhetoric against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>. Its ideology differed. Plínio Salgado read history as a struggle between materialism and spiritualism, the belief in God and the immortality of the soul, and wanted nationalism to be a shared spiritual identity, its Christian virtues enforced by a state that would require political activity under an acknowledged leader. Members were urged to an Internal Revolution, giving up selfish thinking to join a great Integralist family. The movement drew support from military officers, especially in the Brazilian Navy.</p><p>Gustavo Barroso, the party's chief ideologist after Salgado, held militant antisemitic views and was the author of the first Portuguese translation of the Protocols of the Elders of Zion. His views led to serious ruptures in 1935 and 1936, when Salgado almost gave up the leadership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early 1930s President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a>, having crushed the left, turned to the Integralists as a mobilised base of right-wing support. By 1935 the movement claimed a rapidly growing membership, especially among German-Brazilians and Italian-Brazilians. From 1934 Integralists fought street battles with the Communist movement of Luiz Carlos Prestes, and the Communists launched an uprising in 1935. When Vargas took full dictatorial powers in 1937 he turned against the movement, as Salgado's presidential ambitions threatened his hold on power. In 1938 Integralists attacked the Guanabara Palace by night; police and army troops arrived at the last minute, and the gunfight left around twenty casualties. The attempt became known as the Pajama Putsch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The party disintegrated after 1938. In 1945 Salgado founded the Party of Popular Representation, which kept the ideology without the uniforms and salutes. Former Integralists went different ways: some took part in the 1964 coup against President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-goulart">João Goulart</a>, while Goulart's foreign minister Santiago Dantas and Bishop Hélder Câmara moved to the left. Under the military regime Salgado joined the pro-military ARENA, the former Integralists Augusto Rademaker and Márcio Melo sat on the junta that briefly ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1969, and Hélder Câmara was the regime's best-known opponent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brazilian Integralist Action” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politics polarised between the fascist-inspired <b>Integralists</b> and the left-wing National Liberation Alliance, which launched a failed communist uprising in 1935.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-brazilian-republic">Second Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
